# macOS
.DS_Store
._*

# Editor
.vscode/
*.swp

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local-only reports (kept out of version control)
reports/

# Talking-web generated clips
tools/talking_web/_out/

# Virtual environments
.venv/
venv/

# Third-party engines cloned into the repo (multi-GB with weights).
# Leading slash anchors these to the repo root -- without it the pattern also
# matched our own tools/livetalking/ (Windows paths are case-insensitive), which
# silently excluded the launcher scripts from version control.
/GPT-SoVITS/
/LiveTalking/
# CosyVoice 2 — under evaluation as an emotion-controllable alternative to GPT-SoVITS.
# Same reasoning as the two above: multi-GB checkout with its own venv and weights. The
# Windows/cu128 requirements variant IS tracked, in patches/.
/CosyVoice/

# Voice data: source audio + intermediates are large and re-derivable.
# The .list manifest and QC report ARE tracked so a dataset stays reproducible.
kols/*/voice/raw/
kols/*/voice/work/
kols/*/voice/dataset/*.wav

# Avatar build intermediates. base_subtle.mp4 IS tracked (it is small, and it is the
# exact clip the avatar was built from — the built avatar itself is ~237 MB and lives
# under the gitignored LiveTalking/, so without this clip the avatar is not reproducible).
kols/*/avatar/frame.png
kols/*/avatar/base_static.mp4
kols/*/avatar/base_video.mp4
kols/*/avatar/_frames/


# Reply-queue log: operational state (draft/approve/reject decisions), grows over time
# and is specific to a deployment, not to the code. The audit trail belongs on the box
# that made the decisions.
kols/*/replies/
/LivePortrait/

# Studio renders/uploads: generated artefacts, regenerable, and uploads may be
# third-party audio that should not land in the repo.
kols/_studio/

# Rendered voice clips. Regenerable, and the browser on this box cannot play audio, so
# these exist to be opened by hand in a media player.
/renders/

# RVC, cloned in for singing-voice conversion. Same reasoning as the four engines above:
# a multi-GB checkout with its own venv and weights, and reproducible from its upstream.
/RVC/

# The LLM fine-tuning workspace. Everything under it is either downloaded or derived, and
# it is 30 GB: the base checkpoint, a merged fp16 GGUF, the LoRA adapters and a llama.cpp
# checkout. One file alone is 15 GB, and GitHub refuses anything over 100 MB — so `git add -A`
# here does not merely bloat the repository, it produces a push that cannot succeed.
#
# What makes the fine-tune reproducible is tracked instead, and is small: the training and
# validation splits in datasets/, the scripts in tools/llm_train/, and the Modelfile.
/finetune/
!/finetune/export/Modelfile-*

# Song audio — every subdirectory of songs/, keeping only songs.json.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that decides it. They are large: source mixes,
# pitch-shifted intermediates, separated stems and comparison renders, tens of MB each and
# hundreds together. And they are third-party in origin — a converted vocal is indistinguishable
# from its source after conversion, which is exactly why songs.json records `rights` per entry
# at import time. A file whose licence has to be looked up in a manifest is not a file to push
# to a remote by default; the same reasoning already keeps kols/_studio/ out.
#
# songs.json itself IS tracked: it is the library, the rights record, and what makes the audio
# reproducible from its sources.
kols/*/songs/*/

# Runtime logs from the local services.
*.log

# Office lock files, created while a document is open.
~$*

# Per-fan conversation memory written by the one-to-one chat: what she remembers about a named
# person, their thread, and the facts extracted from it. Operational state about individuals,
# and it belongs on the machine that holds the conversation — the same reasoning that keeps
# the reply queue out.
kols/*/fans/

# The RVC training corpus: 320 clips derived from material already tracked or already ignored,
# and rebuildable with tools/tts_train/build_rvc_corpus.py.
kols/*/voice/rvc_corpus/

# Reference-clip intermediates (.orig, .prev, .lufs16, .owner_highpass) — the working steps
# behind the reference clip, not the clip itself.
kols/*/voice/*.wav.*

# The owner's own recorded voice, and the blend derived from it. NOT a size decision: consent
# to use a real person's voice to build a character locally is not consent to publish the
# recording, and this remote's visibility has not been confirmed. Excluded until somebody who
# can answer that says otherwise — `git add -f kols/sofia-vargas/voice/ref_human.wav` is all
# it takes. The synthetic ref.wav, which is what the character was bootstrapped from, is
# tracked and always has been.
kols/*/voice/ref_human.*
kols/*/voice/ref_blend.wav
